# Module README Style

## Scope section convention

- **In scope:** what this module version already delivers.
- **Out of scope:** deferred work still ahead on this module’s product line.
- Do not put permanent non-ownership of other layers (HTTP, sibling domains) under
  Out of scope; put those under Constraints / Responsibilities.

## Module README content

- A feature-module README documents only that module: purpose, responsibilities,
  public surface, local build/run/test, and current scope.
- Do not describe how modules connect, depend on each other, or fit the monorepo
  graph. Cross-module wiring belongs in `ROOT_README.md` (and the product
  overview `README.md` only when a product release updates it).
- Root / product docs may name modules and show dependency/runtime topology.
- A module README may state its own runtime interface (ports, paths, env) without
  narrating sibling modules.

## Style

- Prefer a technical tone: short declarative sentences, tables, lists, code
  identifiers.
- Avoid literary framing, storytelling, motivational “why we might later…”, and
  essay-like paragraphs.
- Lead with what the module is and what it does.
- Do not open a README by listing what the module lacks (frameworks, UI, etc.).
  State absences only where they define a real boundary, and keep that brief.
- Keep “out of scope” brief and factual: deferred work for this module’s line
  (what is not done yet), not permanent ownership of sibling layers.
- Permanent module boundaries belong under Constraints / Responsibilities, not
  under Out of scope.
- Prefer precise terms (`LibraryProfile`, `WatchEvent`, `/api/v1/dashboard`) over
  metaphors.
- Write module READMEs in English (product `README.md` `### Цель` is the only
  Russian exception; see `.cursor/rules/doc-language.mdc`).
